---
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import heroImage from "../../assets/images/hero-sessions.avif";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

const asset = (path: string) => withBase(`/resources/the-last-one/${path}`);
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Production walkthrough: The Last One"
  description="A real production record showing how one AI short film moved from directing brief and screenplay to references, generated shots, rough cuts, sound and a final scoped cut."
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="An abstract geometric composition on warm cream: two large overlapping warm-gold and black circles at center-left crossed by a thin black frame, a small muted dark-green square isolated at upper right"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p class="guide-intro">
    <strong>The Last One</strong> is a real production walkthrough, not a model
    answer. It follows the same direct → write → visualise → plan → generate →
    evaluate → redirect → edit → sound sequence used in the workshops. The
    production was originally planned as 47 shots, then deliberately scoped to
    the 21 shots that could be completed in the available time. Nothing below
    stands in for work that was not made.
  </p>

  <nav class="stage-nav" aria-label="Production stages">
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#week-1">Direct</a></li>
      <li><a href="#week-2">Write</a></li>
      <li><a href="#week-4">Plan</a></li>
      <li><a href="#week-5">Visualise</a></li>
      <li><a href="#week-7">Generate</a></li>
      <li><a href="#week-8">Evaluate</a></li>
      <li><a href="#week-9">Assemble</a></li>
      <li><a href="#week-10">Edit</a></li>
      <li><a href="#week-11">Sound</a></li>
      <li><a href="#week-12">Review</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <section id="week-1">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 1 · Direct</p>
    <h2>Start with an intention, not a tool</h2>
    <p>
      The directing brief was deliberately simple: the first minute must read
      as a serious post-apocalyptic film, while the comedy arrives from an
      ordinary delivery rider entering that world without changing his everyday
      behaviour. Performances stay restrained; the joke comes from the collision
      between those two realities.
    </p>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Write one sentence describing the audience's initial reading, then one sentence naming what later changes that reading.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A fixed tonal intention that every shot, performance and sound decision can be judged against.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-2">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 2 · Story</p>
    <h2>Reduce the idea to observable pressure</h2>
    <p>
      A survivor believes he and Lucky are the last living beings. A knock at
      the bunker promises human contact, but the visitor is a delivery rider
      completing an absurdly late order. The practical story question is not
      “is the apocalypse real?” but “how long can the film preserve the
      survivor's belief before ordinary life breaks it?”
    </p>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Name the protagonist's belief, the event that challenges it and the immediate change the audience should observe.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A one-paragraph premise that can be staged without explanatory narration.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-3">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 3 · Screenplay</p>
    <h2>Write what a camera and microphone can record</h2>
    <p>
      The screenplay turns the premise into actions: the survivor searches,
      marks the last area on a map, shares food with Lucky, hears a knock and
      approaches the door. Dialogue is withheld until the visual pattern is
      established, so “your delivery” can interrupt a serious performance
      instead of announcing the joke early.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>“The survivor eats a spoonful of chickpeas and forces himself to chew. ‘No delivery either.’ The moment he finishes, a heavy knock lands on the iron door.”</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      This excerpt does three jobs without explaining the theme: it establishes
      deprivation, plants the delivery idea in dialogue, and turns the knock
      into both suspense and setup.
    </p>
    <p><a href={asset("docs/screenplay.md")}>Read the working screenplay</a>.</p>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Underline every sentence that cannot be shown or heard, then rewrite it as behaviour, staging or dialogue.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A directable scene whose tonal turn happens through an observable event.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-4">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 4 · Shot design</p>
    <h2>Give every shot one job</h2>
    <p>
      The production plan grew to 47 shots. Each row names duration, action,
      sound and asset status, while the first 21 became the completed production
      scope. The plan also records removed material rather than quietly
      renumbering it, so the reason for every gap remains visible.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p><strong>Shot 14 · 8 seconds:</strong> Close on the survivor. His rifle lowers and his eyes fill with hope. “Three years… I finally—” is interrupted from outside: “Order ending 3721? Your delivery.”</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The row identifies the visible change, exact interruption and intended
      duration. It can therefore guide generation, editing and later review;
      “the reveal shot” would not be specific enough.
    </p>
    <p><a href={asset("docs/shot-list.md")}>Open the complete shot list</a>.</p>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> For each beat, state what changes between the first and last frame and why that change deserves its own shot.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A numbered list with framing, action, sound, duration and a single dramatic purpose per shot.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-5">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 5 · World bible</p>
    <h2>Lock identity before asking for motion</h2>
    <p>
      Character sheets lock the survivor, Lucky and the rider. Location
      references lock the bunker interior, its exterior and the abandoned
      village. These images describe repeatable identity; individual keyframes
      are still free to change pose, expression and camera position.
    </p>
    <div class="reference-grid">
      <figure><img src={asset("images/main-character-reference.webp")} alt="Reference sheet for the survivor, showing his face, clothing, red scarf and equipment" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Survivor</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={asset("images/lucky-reference.webp")} alt="Reference sheet for Lucky, a yellow-and-white dog with upright ears and a worn red collar" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lucky</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={asset("images/delivery-rider-reference.webp")} alt="Reference sheet for the delivery rider in a yellow helmet and uniform" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Delivery rider</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={asset("images/bunker-interior-reference.webp")} alt="Reference sheet for the bunker interior with map, radio, table, shelves and iron door" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bunker interior</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={asset("images/bunker-exterior-reference.webp")} alt="Reference sheet for the bunker exterior, road and surrounding mountains" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bunker exterior</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src={asset("images/abandoned-village-reference.webp")} alt="Reference sheet for the abandoned village and surrounding wasteland" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Abandoned village</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Separate fixed identity from changing shot state. Label every reference and version.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A small, approved reference set that another person could use without inventing missing details.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-6">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 6 · Production order</p>
    <h2>Generate in dependency order</h2>
    <p>
      The project did not treat 47 rows as 47 independent requests. Character
      and location references came first; a shot's approved keyframe preceded
      its motion pass; and a previous shot's final frame was used where an
      action or screen direction needed to continue. Text on phones and other
      fragile interface details were reserved for post-production.
    </p>
    <div class="dependency-flow" aria-label="Production dependency order">
      <div><span>1</span><strong>Lock identity</strong><small>Character and location sheets</small></div>
      <div><span>2</span><strong>Compose the anchor</strong><small>One approved keyframe</small></div>
      <div><span>3</span><strong>Direct motion</strong><small>Action, timing and camera response</small></div>
      <div><span>4</span><strong>Inspect the take</strong><small>Continuity, anatomy, performance and sound</small></div>
      <div><span>5</span><strong>Pass continuity forward</strong><small>Use the approved end frame for the next dependency</small></div>
    </div>
    <h3>How the order worked in practice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Shots 12–14:</strong> the closed iron door and screen direction were locked before the rider was revealed.</li>
      <li><strong>Shots 15–16:</strong> the end of the survivor's reaction became the start condition for opening the door.</li>
      <li><strong>Shots 20–21:</strong> the three-character doorway composition was preserved while the dramatic focus moved from the rider to Lucky.</li>
      <li><strong>Phone text:</strong> the device stayed in the rider's hand, but readable interface text was deferred to post-production.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Mark each shot's dependencies, then move blocked shots later instead of generating around an unresolved reference.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A production order that explains why one shot must exist before another can begin.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-7">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 7 · Anchor to motion</p>
    <h2>Direct one visible action from a locked frame</h2>
    <p>
      Shot 20 begins from a three-character doorway composition. The direction
      asks the survivor to grab the rider's shoulders urgently without turning
      the action into an attack, while the rider retains the phone and bag and
      Lucky stays present below them.
    </p>
    <div class="media-pair">
      <figure><img src={asset("images/shot-20-keyframe.webp")} alt="Shot 20 keyframe: the survivor grips the delivery rider's shoulders at the bunker doorway while Lucky watches" loading="lazy" /><figcaption><span class="at-tag">Anchor</span> Shot 20 approved keyframe</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><video src={asset("videos/shot-20.mp4")} controls preload="metadata" playsinline aria-label="Generated Shot 20 in which the survivor asks the rider how many people remain"></video><figcaption><span class="at-tag">Motion</span> Generated result</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="example-grid">
      <article>
        <p class="at-tag">Example A · performance</p>
        <h3>Urgency without aggression</h3>
        <p>“He grips the rider's shoulders urgently, but does not shake, strike or threaten him. The rider keeps hold of the phone and delivery bag.”</p>
        <p><strong>Why it works:</strong> the note names both the desired action and the nearest unwanted interpretation.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="at-tag">Example B · camera</p>
        <h3>Movement caused by attention</h3>
        <p>“When the survivor turns toward Lucky, the camera follows slightly later with a restrained horizontal adjustment of about 10–15 degrees.”</p>
        <p><strong>Why it works:</strong> the camera has a dramatic cause, direction and limit.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="at-tag">Example C · continuity</p>
        <h3>Protect what is already approved</h3>
        <p>“Keep the rider at the doorway, Lucky between both men, the phone in the rider's right hand, and the delivery bag on the same side.”</p>
        <p><strong>Why it works:</strong> continuity constraints are concrete enough to inspect in the result.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p><a href={asset("docs/prompt-library.md")}>Read the production prompt library</a>.</p>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Describe timing, physical action and what must remain unchanged. Avoid mood-only direction.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> One motion request whose success can be judged from visible behaviour.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-8">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 8 · Evaluate and redirect</p>
    <h2>Change the diagnosed failure, not the whole shot</h2>
    <p>
      Shot 21's first keyframe did not make the survivor–Lucky interaction
      legible enough. The revision changed only that relationship: the survivor
      pets Lucky's head and Lucky turns to meet his gaze. The rider, doorway,
      costumes, props, scale and light remained locked.
    </p>
    <div class="media-pair">
      <figure><img src={asset("images/shot-21-keyframe.webp")} alt="Revised Shot 21 keyframe: the survivor pets Lucky's head and Lucky looks up at him while the rider remains at the doorway" loading="lazy" /><figcaption><span class="at-tag at-tag--selected">Selected</span> Revised interaction</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><video src={asset("videos/shot-21.mp4")} controls preload="metadata" playsinline aria-label="Generated Shot 21 in which the survivor turns to Lucky after learning other people remain"></video><figcaption><span class="at-tag">Result</span> Produced take</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Name the observable mismatch, preserve everything that already works and rewrite only the failing action.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A revision note precise enough to compare the next result against the previous one.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-9">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 9 · Assembly</p>
    <h2>Let the sequence reveal continuity problems</h2>
    <p>
      The first assembly retained every available clip and used hard cuts. It
      exposed repeated dialogue at shot boundaries: “who?” appeared twice, and
      the delivery number was repeated across adjacent clips. Those are edit
      and continuity findings produced by the assembly, not faults invented in
      advance.
    </p>
    <p><a href={asset("docs/rough-cut-notes.md")}>Read the rough-cut record</a>.</p>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Watch the assembly unattended and log each break by shot number before proposing a repair.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A continuity log separating what will be fixed from what will be deliberately accepted.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-10">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 10 · Edit</p>
    <h2>Make the second cut answer a specific diagnosis</h2>
    <p>
      Rough Cut v02 trims about 0.9 seconds from the start of Shot 13, keeps the
      first delivery-number line and replaces the repeated dialogue under Shots
      15–16 with an environmental bed. Compare versions for those exact
      decisions rather than asking which one merely “feels better.”
    </p>
    <div class="media-pair">
      <figure><video src={asset("videos/rough-cut-v01.mp4")} controls preload="metadata" playsinline aria-label="The Last One rough cut version one, retaining the original shot boundaries"></video><figcaption><span class="at-tag at-tag--rejected">Before</span> Rough Cut v01</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><video src={asset("videos/rough-cut-v02.mp4")} controls preload="metadata" playsinline aria-label="The Last One rough cut version two, with repeated dialogue removed"></video><figcaption><span class="at-tag at-tag--selected">After</span> Rough Cut v02</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Make one cut that removes repetition, then state what information or pause the shorter version preserves.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> Two reviewable edits and a sentence identifying their exact difference.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-11">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 11 · Sound</p>
    <h2>Use sound to protect the tonal turn</h2>
    <p>
      The opening gained an interrupted emergency broadcast, radio filtering
      and a restrained post-apocalyptic music bed. The music stops after the
      final knock so the ordinary delivery dialogue arrives without an
      emotional instruction underneath it. English subtitles were prepared as
      a separate SRT and a burned-in review export rather than generated inside
      the image.
    </p>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> Identify where music makes a claim, where it should yield to dialogue and which sounds must remain editable as separate layers.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A sound pass whose cue timing can be defended beat by beat.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="week-12">
    <p class="at-tag">Week 12 · Review and scope</p>
    <h2>Finish the version that can be finished honestly</h2>
    <p>
      Time did not allow Shots 22–47 to be produced. The final scoped cut ends
      at Shot 21 rather than filling the unmade second half with placeholders or
      presenting planned material as completed work. That constraint becomes a
      production decision: preserve one coherent, reviewable chain from brief
      to final sound instead of hiding an unfinished expansion.
    </p>
    <figure class="final-cut">
      <video src={asset("videos/final-cut-v01.mp4")} controls preload="metadata" playsinline aria-label="Final scoped cut of The Last One, covering Shots 1 through 21 with broadcast, music, dialogue and sound"></video>
      <figcaption><span class="at-tag at-tag--selected">Final scoped cut</span> Shots 1–21 · 1 minute 26 seconds</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="workshop-step"><p><strong>Action:</strong> State what is complete, what was removed and why the remaining scope still demonstrates the intended directing process.</p><p><strong>Output:</strong> A clearly versioned export and an honest record of the production boundary.</p></div>
  </section>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  section { scroll-margin-top: 2rem; }
  .guide-intro { font-size: 1.1rem; }
  .stage-nav ol { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .stage-nav a { display: inline-block; padding: 0.35rem 0.7rem; border: 1px solid var(--at-border); border-radius: 999px; }
  .reference-grid, .media-pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--at-spacing-lg, 1.5rem); margin-block: var(--at-spacing-lg, 1.5rem); }
  figure { min-width: 0; margin: 0; }
  figure img, figure video { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--at-border); border-radius: var(--at-border-radius); background: #000; }
  figcaption { margin-top: 0.5rem; color: var(--at-text-secondary); font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm); }
  .workshop-step { margin-block: var(--at-spacing-lg, 1.5rem); padding: var(--at-spacing-md, 1rem); border-left: 0.25rem solid var(--at-primary); background: var(--at-bg-alt); }
  .workshop-step p { margin-block: 0.35rem; }
  blockquote { margin-block: var(--at-spacing-lg, 1.5rem); padding: 0.25rem 1rem; border-left: 0.25rem solid var(--at-primary); color: var(--at-text-secondary); }
  .dependency-flow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 0.75rem; margin-block: var(--at-spacing-lg, 1.5rem); }
  .dependency-flow div, .example-grid article { padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--at-border); border-radius: var(--at-border-radius); background: var(--at-bg-alt); }
  .dependency-flow span { display: block; width: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 0.65rem; border-radius: 999px; text-align: center; background: var(--at-primary); color: var(--at-bg); font-weight: 700; }
  .dependency-flow strong, .dependency-flow small { display: block; }
  .dependency-flow small { margin-top: 0.35rem; color: var(--at-text-secondary); }
  .example-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-block: var(--at-spacing-lg, 1.5rem); }
  .example-grid h3 { margin-top: 0.5rem; }
  .final-cut { margin-block: var(--at-spacing-lg, 1.5rem); }
  @media (max-width: 60rem) { .dependency-flow { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } .example-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  @media (max-width: 48rem) { .reference-grid, .media-pair, .dependency-flow { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
